      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263946",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements is false?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Every cyclic group is abelian group</li>\n    <li>Every abelian group is cyclic group</li>\n    <li>Let $G$ be a group and let $H_1$ and $H_2$ be subgroups of $G$ such that $H_1⊄H_2$ and $H_2⊄H_1$ the union $H_1\\cup H_2$ is never a subgroup</li>\n    <li>A group&nbsp;$G$&nbsp;has an element&nbsp;$g$&nbsp;such that&nbsp;$G_{23}=e$&nbsp;then there can not exist an integer&nbsp;$0&lt;x&lt;23$&nbsp;such that&nbsp;$gx=e$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263945",
          "text": "Let $(G, \\Box)$ be a group where $\\Box$ is defined as $x \\Box y = x+y+1$. The inverse of $3$ is _________ ?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "-5",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,numerical-answers,groups",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263944",
          "text": "<p>Let $(G,\\Box )$ be a group defined as $x\\Box y =&nbsp;x+yxy+1;&nbsp;x\\in R,&nbsp;−1&lt;x&lt;1$. Which of the following statements is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$G$ is a semi group but not a&nbsp;monoid</li>\n    <li>$G$ is a&nbsp;monoid but not a group</li>\n    <li>$G$ is a group but not an abelian&nbsp;group</li>\n    <li>$G$ is an abelian group</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,groups",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263943",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following statements about Group $(G,*)$ ?<br>\n$P: a^{2}=e \\implies a=e$</p>\n\n<p>$Q:&nbsp;&nbsp;a*a=b*b \\implies a=b$</p>\n\n<p>$R:&nbsp;(a*b)^{2}=a^{2}*b^{2}$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the above&nbsp;statements is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only $P$</li>\n    <li>Only $P$ and $Q$</li>\n    <li>Only $P$ and $R$</li>\n    <li>All of the $P$, $Q$ and $R$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,groups",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263942",
          "text": "<p>Let $G$ be a group. It is given that $abc = e$.&nbsp;Consider the following statements.</p>\n\n<p>$P: cab = e$</p>\n\n<p>$Q:&nbsp; bca = e$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the above&nbsp;statements is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only $P$</li>\n    <li>Only $Q$</li>\n    <li>Both $P$ and $Q$</li>\n    <li>None</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,groups",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263941",
          "text": "<p>Let $G$ be a group.&nbsp;$a,b,c\\in G$. Which of the following statements in TRUE regarding $G?$</p>\n\n<p>$P: \\text{ If } ab=ca \\implies&nbsp;b=c,&nbsp;G \\text{&nbsp;is abelian group}$</p>\n\n<p>$Q:&nbsp;(a^{−1}ba)^{n}&nbsp;=&nbsp;a^{−1} b^{n} a$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only $P$</li>\n    <li>Only $Q$</li>\n    <li>Both $P$ and $Q$</li>\n    <li>Neither $P$ nor $Q$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,groups,abelian-group",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263940",
          "text": "<p>Let $G$ be a finite abelian group, say, $G=\\{e,a_{1},a_{2},\\ldots,a_{n}\\}$. Which of the following statements is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li>$S1: (a_{1}a_{2} \\ldots a_{n})^{2}=e$</li>\n    <li>$S2: \\text{If there is no element }x \\neq e&nbsp;\\text{ in } G&nbsp;\\text {such that } x=x^{−1}, \\text{then }(a_{1}a_{2}\\ldots a_{n})=e.$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only $S1$</li>\n    <li>Only $S2$</li>\n    <li>Both $S1$ and $S2$</li>\n    <li>Neither $S1$ nor $S2$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263939",
          "text": "<p>Let $G = \\langle\\{e,a,b\\}, *\\rangle $ be a group.</p>\n\n<p>$$\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\\textbf{*}&amp;\\textbf{e}&amp;\\textbf{a}&amp;\\textbf{b}\\\\ \\hline&nbsp; \\textbf{e}&amp;e&amp;a&amp;b\\\\\\textbf{a}&amp;a&amp;\\boxed{}&amp;\\boxed{}\\\\ \\textbf{b} &amp;b&nbsp;&amp;\\boxed{}<br>\n&amp;\\boxed{}\\end{array}$$</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>The number of ways this Cayley table can be filled so that the resulting table represents a Group?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$1$</li>\n    <li>$2$</li>\n    <li>$16$</li>\n    <li>$8$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,groups",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263938",
          "text": "If $G$ is a cyclic group of order $32$ and $a^{2002}=a^{n}$ where $a\\in G$ and $0\\leq n<32$, the value of $n$ is ________",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "18",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4,numerical-answers,groups",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263937",
          "text": "<p>Which of the above lattice is distributive?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:lower-alpha\">\n    <li><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></li>\n    <li><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><br>\n&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only a</li>\n    <li>Only b</li>\n    <li>Both a and b</li>\n    <li>None</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263936",
          "text": "<p>If $(A_{1},\\leq 1)$ and $(A_{2}, \\leq 2)$ are two posets, then the&nbsp;dictionary order&nbsp;$\\leq$ on $A=A_{1} \\times&nbsp; A_{2}$&nbsp;is given by $(x1,x2)&nbsp; \\leq (y1,y2)$&nbsp;if&nbsp;$x_{1} &lt; _{1}y_{1} \\vee (x_{1}=y_{1} \\wedge x_{2} \\leq _{2}y_{2})$. Which of the following is true about the Cartesian product $A=A1 \\times A2$ of two posets&nbsp;$A1$ and $A2$ under the dictionary order $\\leq$</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>reflexive but not anti symmetric</li>\n    <li>anti symmetric but not reflexive</li>\n    <li>reflexive and anti symmetric but not symmetric</li>\n    <li>poset</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263935",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements is&nbsp;FALSE&nbsp;about a poset&nbsp;$(Z, \\leq)$?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$Z$&nbsp;is a lattice</li>\n    <li>Every subset of&nbsp;$Z$&nbsp;has an upper bound</li>\n    <li>For some subsets of&nbsp;$Z$&nbsp;there is no lower bound</li>\n    <li>$Z$&nbsp;is an unbounded lattice</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263934",
          "text": "<p>Let $(D_{n}, \\mid )$ denote the poset of all divisors of $n$, where $n$ is a positive integer. For all&nbsp;$a,b&nbsp;\\in D_{n} \\: a \\vee b$ and $ a \\wedge b$ is</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$\\text{gcd}(a,b) \\text{ and } \\text{lcm}(a,b)$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{lcm}(a,b) \\text{ and } \\text{gcd}(a,b)$</li>\n    <li>$a.b \\text{ and } a+b$</li>\n    <li>$a+b \\text{ and } a.b$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263933",
          "text": "<p>If a boolean lattice is totally ordered then the maximum number of elements it can have is?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$1$</li>\n    <li>$2$</li>\n    <li>$3$</li>\n    <li>$100$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "263932",
          "text": "<p>The number of partial order relations on set of $3$ elements $S = \\{1,2,3 \\}$?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$10$</li>\n    <li>$6$</li>\n    <li>$19$</li>\n    <li>$5$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm4",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}